1. Understanding the MVC Architecture
Model

    Represents the application’s data (e.g., Employee entity).
    Maps directly to the database table using JPA and Hibernate.
    Uses annotations like @Entity, @Id, and @Column to define the structure.

View

    Not relevant here as this is a backend-only REST API. In a full-stack application, the frontend (React, Angular, etc.) serves as the view.

Controller

    Manages the HTTP requests (REST endpoints) and communicates with the Service Layer.
    Annotated with @RestController and @RequestMapping.

Service

    Contains business logic and calls the Repository to fetch or save data.
    Annotated with @Service.

Repository

    Handles database interactions using Spring Data JPA.
    Annotated with @Repository.

2. Step-by-Step Flow
Step 1: HTTP Request Handling (Controller Layer)

    HTTP requests are sent by the client (Postman, frontend, etc.).
        Example: A POST request to /employees with JSON payload to create an employee.
    The EmployeeController receives the request via methods annotated with @PostMapping, @GetMapping, etc.
    The request payload is mapped to the Employee object using @RequestBody.

Controller Example

@RestController
@RequestMapping("/employees")
public class EmployeeController {
    private final EmployeeService employeeService;

    @Autowired
    public EmployeeController(EmployeeService employeeService) {
        this.employeeService = employeeService;
    }

    @PostMapping
    public ResponseEntity<Employee> createEmployee(@RequestBody Employee employee) {
        Employee savedEmployee = employeeService.save(employee); // Calls Service Layer
        return new ResponseEntity<>(savedEmployee, HttpStatus.CREATED);
    }
}

Step 2: Business Logic (Service Layer)

    The Controller calls the Service layer method, passing the request data (Employee object).
    The Service layer contains the core business logic.
        Example: Calculating salary hikes, validating input, handling exceptions.
    It interacts with the Repository to perform database operations like save, update, or delete.

Service Example

@Service
public class EmployeeService {
    private final EmployeeRepository employeeRepository;

    @Autowired
    public EmployeeService(EmployeeRepository employeeRepository) {
        this.employeeRepository = employeeRepository;
    }

    public Employee save(Employee employee) {
        return employeeRepository.save(employee); // Saves Employee to DB
    }

    public void delete(Long id) {
        employeeRepository.deleteById(id); // Deletes Employee by ID
    }

    public Employee applyHike(Long id, Double percentage) {
        Employee employee = employeeRepository.findById(id)
            .orElseThrow(() -> new ResourceNotFoundException("Employee not found"));
        employee.setSalary(employee.getSalary() * (1 + percentage / 100)); // Hike Logic
        return employeeRepository.save(employee); // Save Updated Employee
    }
}

Step 3: Database Interaction (Repository Layer)

    The Repository layer interacts with the database.
    Spring Data JPA abstracts SQL queries. Methods like save, findById, and deleteById are available without additional coding.
    Custom queries can be added using @Query or method naming conventions.

Repository Example

@Repository
public interface EmployeeRepository extends JpaRepository<Employee, Long> {
    // JpaRepository provides built-in methods like save, findById, deleteById
}

Step 4: Data Storage (Model Layer)

    The Employee class represents the database structure and business model.
    It is mapped to a MySQL table using JPA annotations like @Entity, @Table, etc.

Model Example

@Entity
@Table(name = "employees")
public class Employee {
    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
    private Long id;

    @Column(nullable = false, unique = true)
    private String email;

    @Column(nullable = false)
    private String role;

    @Column(nullable = false)
    private Double salary;

    @Embedded
    private Address address; // Composite Object

    // Getters, Setters, and Constructors
}

3. Exception Handling

Handled using a global exception handler with @ControllerAdvice. This ensures that all exceptions are processed consistently.
Example

@ControllerAdvice
public class GlobalExceptionHandler {
    @ExceptionHandler(ResourceNotFoundException.class)
    public ResponseEntity<String> handleResourceNotFound(ResourceNotFoundException ex) {
        return new ResponseEntity<>(ex.getMessage(), HttpStatus.NOT_FOUND);
    }
}

4. Logging

Logging tracks changes and actions in the application:

    Using AOP (LoggingAspect), logs are added before and after methods like save, delete, and applyHike.
    This ensures detailed tracking without polluting the core business logic.

Aspect Example

@Aspect
@Component
public class LoggingAspect {
    private static final Logger logger = LoggerFactory.getLogger(LoggingAspect.class);

    @Before("execution(* com.example.employee_management.service.EmployeeService.save(..))")
    public void logBeforeSave(Object employee) {
        logger.info("Preparing to save employee: {}", employee);
    }

    @AfterReturning(pointcut = "execution(* com.example.employee_management.service.EmployeeService.save(..))", returning = "result")
    public void logAfterSave(Object result) {
        logger.info("Employee saved successfully: {}", result);
    }
}

5. Flow Overview

    Client sends an HTTP request to the controller (e.g., POST /employees).
    The controller maps the request payload to an object and calls the service.
    The service processes the request, applies business logic, and interacts with the repository.
    The repository performs CRUD operations on the database.
    Response is returned to the client with the result.

6. Important Annotations and Interview Relevance
Spring Core

    @Autowired: Dependency injection for services or repositories.
    @Component: Indicates a Spring Bean.
    @Service: Specialized @Component for the service layer.
    @Repository: Specialized @Component for the repository layer.

Spring Boot

    @SpringBootApplication: Combines @Configuration, @EnableAutoConfiguration, and @ComponentScan.

Spring MVC

    @RestController: Combines @Controller and @ResponseBody to return JSON responses.
    @RequestMapping: Base path for controller methods.
    @GetMapping, @PostMapping, @DeleteMapping, @PutMapping: Handle HTTP methods.
    @RequestBody: Maps JSON payload to an object.
    @PathVariable: Maps URL path variables.
    @RequestParam: Maps query parameters.

Spring Data JPA

    @Entity: Defines a JPA entity.
    @Table: Maps a class to a database table.
    @Id, @GeneratedValue: Define primary key and auto-generation strategy.
    @Embedded: Used for composite objects.

Spring AOP

    @Aspect: Indicates an Aspect class.
    @Before, @AfterReturning: Define advice for methods.